Hi All!

I am happy to announce that XBanner v0.04 has been released! I have just
uploaded it to the /pub/Linux/Incoming directory of sunsite.unc.edu (March
17th 21:00 GMT). It will soon be moved to /pub/Linux/X11/xutils.

So what's new?
- --------------
* The StandOut/StandIn effects are done. They give stunning results. I didn't
  think it would look that good!
* There are 2 brand-new rendering effects. Both are by-products of the
  method used to create the StandIn/Out effects. They are:
  a) PopArt - multiple outlines with alternating colors.
  b) Coin   - A StandOut effect which adds a very thin rim to the letters
	      giving them the same appearance as coins.
* Lots of behaviour fixes, some bug fixes, and hopefully no new bugs.

What does it do?
- ----------------
In general, XBanner takes a line of text, and draws it onto the root window,
rendering some possible effects. You can choose the text, the font (which
better be scalable so you can choose a really big font), the effect (shadow,
outline, thickness, plus more), the colors, etc. And of course - where on
the screen it will be located.

The main reason I wrote it was those nice login screens that some commercial
operating systems have... Like DEC's Alpha new login screen, and HP's login.
So I said "Why shouldn't linux have one?".

What you need:
- --------------
X11R5 or higher installed, with XDM being your display manager. This means
that XBanner was not meant to be used by people who use virtual consoles to
log in, and then start up the X Window System manually.

In the file XBanner0.04.tar.gz you will find a sample configuration file,
which will draw a nice "Linux!" banner on your root window. You can run this
with no problems while in a regular session, but XBanner was meant to be
run from the XDM script that sets up the screen before login. Usually this
is /usr/lib/X11/xdm/Xsetup_0 ...

The README and xbanner.doc are very detailed and will get you all the bits
of information you need to install, configure and use. The configuration
file is also heavily commented to help out.

