              ISO/IEC 9899:1990 (C standard) conformant MATH LIBRARY

                                        for 

                                     ELF Linux


Open Linux Ltd are delighted to release a new and standard conformant MATH
library for Linux. 

Developed for ELF Linux (it is not supported on a.out systems), this new
libm cures a number of existing problems whilst providing *true* conformance
to the C standard ISO/IEC 9899:1990 and also to XPG4.

During the implementation of the C Standard for Linux-FT, we found
that the standard Linux math library had too many problems to be
worth rectifying, so a new library was developed. At this time we were
solely interested in C Standard (and XPG4) Conformance so all functions not 
covered by the standard were taken from the existing Linux math library 
and fixed as required.

If you are worried about the library that you are using with your Linux, 
we have provided a test suite to:

    -	Validate the new libm when you build it on your system
    -	Validate and report any defects in the libm that you are using.

Known problems with the latest standard Linux math library:

For extreme values, functions return wrong results. E.g., with the standard
Linux math library fmod(5.0,0.0) = 5.0,  pow(-1.0,1.0e20) = NaN, several
functions overflow early though the result could be represented, and only few
functions do correctly set errno to ERANGE on overflow. Some functions like
asin() clobber the floating point stack, an especially dangerous bug because
the function itself returns the correct result, and bad values may be
computed much later.

This enhanced math library
  - returns correct results for all valid input values;

  - sets errno correctly on overflow or domain error and correctly returns
    Inf, -Inf or NaN;

  - knows how to compute even with +/-Inf and NaN;

  - contains all functions not only with "double" type but also with "float"
    and "long double" type;

  - contains all functions as inline assembler code.


Full details of the problems that this new library solves, its Copyright
and copies of the actual library(!!) can be obtained from :

