Helsinki, Finland (Linux News).  After several weeks of surgery, the Son 
of the LSM Robot is back and stronger than ever.  Now running on a 
Linux/Alpha system, it can process several entries per hour without 
disturbing the use of the system as a terminal emulator, using the 
built-in console VT220 terminal emulation. 

``The surgery was extremely successful'', says Dr. Wirzenius, who was 
part of the medical team, ``Sonny now runs on a system that responds 
intelligently to df commands, among other things.  The only problem is a 
slight head-ache caused by the name.''

Sonny regrets that he has not been able to process any of the mail sent 
to him during the past three weeks or so, but he welcomes it if you wish 
to send it again.  In fact, he eagerly looks forward to it.

Fan mail and new Linux Software Map entries can be sent to 
lsm@osf-alpha.cs.helsinki.fi (or lsm@linux-alpha.cs.helsinki.fi, which is 
the same machine under the new name; the old name will remain and should 
work splendidly).

If Sonny's condition seems to get worse, please don't hesitate to mail 
Dr. Wirzenius at liw@iki.fi.

Meanwhile, a new release of the ever-growing LSM master database should 
be out later this week.  Work on automating release engineering is also 
commencing, which may mean that updates can actually happen every week.
``Look, Ma, no hands'', as Dr. Wirzenius colorfully expresses it.

